<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Like Math! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I Like School!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Math!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Engage in basic interactive games with instructions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of words related to school activities by responding with gestures, actions, or simple words in listening games like 'Bingo' (e.g., 'Show me what you like: games or puzzles?', and the students point to or say puzzles or games).
            Can engage with instructions during interactive games (e.g., 'Point to your book.').
            Can respond to simple oral prompts with one or two words related to school activities (e.g., 'Do you like games or math?' 'I like games.' 'I like math.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by playing a simple "Listen and Do" game. The teacher says, "Touch your nose!" or "Stand up!" to get students engaged. Then, the teacher shows large flashcards of school activities like 'math', 'art', 'music', 'playground'. The teacher points to 'math' and says, "Math!" and does a counting motion. The teacher points to 'games' (using a picture of children playing) and says, "Games!" and does a jumping motion. Students repeat the word and action after the teacher. This activates prior knowledge of school and introduces key vocabulary. </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the phrase "I like math!" while pointing to the math flashcard and making a happy face, exaggerating the /l/ sound in 'like' and the /g/ sound in 'games' when presenting the 'games' flashcard. The teacher then models "I like games!" with the corresponding flashcard and action. The teacher also models "I don't like math!" with a sad face and shaking head. Students are encouraged to listen carefully to the words and sounds.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses concept checking questions to ensure comprehension of the new vocabulary and phrases, focusing on listening for understanding.
            </p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the 'math' flashcard) "Is this music or math?" (Students nod for math or shake head for music).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to 'games' flashcard, then 'art' flashcard) "Do I like games?" (Teacher nods happily). "Do I like art?" (Teacher shakes head sadly). "Yes or No?" (Students respond with 'Yes' or 'No' or a corresponding gesture).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple audio story or song (e.g., "What Do You Like at School?") with accompanying large, colorful picture flashcards depicting 'teacher', 'art', 'playground', 'science', 'music', 'math', 'English'. The material should use the grammar "I like..." and "I don't like..." and include simple Yes/No questions. Genre: Simple narrative song/chant. Register: Informal, child-friendly. Format: Audio with visual support.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing likes and dislikes: Use simple phrases like 'I like...' or 'I don't like...' to express preferences. Answering preference questions: Respond to 'Do you like it?' with 'yes' or 'no'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio story/song once. Students listen.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question (Listen and Point):</b> The teacher asks, "What did the children like at school?" and holds up the flashcards. Students listen for words from the song and point to the corresponding flashcards (e.g., 'math', 'music', 'playground').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays the audio again. This time, students listen for specific likes/dislikes.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Listen and show thumbs):</b> The teacher says a statement (e.g., "The boy likes art!" or "The girl doesn't like math!") and students show thumbs up for 'True' or thumbs down for 'False' based on what they hear in the audio.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for comprehension of specific words and phrases related to school activities and expressing preferences. Grammar: Subject + verb ('I like school.'), Present simple ('I like games.'), Yes/No questions ('Do you like it?' 'Yes.'). Vocabulary: teacher, art, playground, science, music, math, English, enjoy, choose, create, count, build, sing, share, look, write, color, interesting, funny, boring, exciting, happy, sad, favorite, colorful. Pronunciation: /l/ in 'like', /g/ in 'games'. Phonemic Awareness: Recognizing the sounds of 'm' in 'math' and 'g' in 'games'.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. 'Bingo' Listening Game (Controlled Practice):</b> Each student receives a 'Bingo' card with pictures of 6-8 different school activities (e.g., 'math', 'art', 'music', 'playground', 'English', 'teacher'). The teacher calls out, "I like math!" or "Show me 'games'!" or "Count the pencils!". Students listen and place a counter on the matching picture on their card. The first student to get three in a row shouts "Bingo!" This activity directly practices listening for target vocabulary and specific instructions while reinforcing phonemic awareness of 'math' and 'games'.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. 'Listen and Choose' (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> The teacher holds up two flashcards, for example, 'math' and 'art'. The teacher asks, "Do you like math or art?" Students listen to the question and point to the activity they prefer. The teacher then models, "I like math!" or "I like art!" and students chorally repeat. The teacher repeats with different pairs of flashcards (e.g., 'music' and 'playground', 'English' and 'science'), allowing students to choose and respond non-verbally first, then with a simple 'Yes' or 'No' and a one-word answer.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. 'Simon Says' (Interactive Listening):</b> The teacher leads a game of "Simon Says" using actions related to school activities and verbs. Examples: "Simon says, 'Color the picture!'" (students pretend to color), "Simon says, 'Sing a song!'" (students sing a note), "Simon says, 'Count your fingers!'" (students count). The teacher also includes phrases for preferences: "Simon says, 'Show me what you like: games or math!'" (Students point to the corresponding flashcard or make a 'counting' or 'playing' gesture). This practices listening to instructions and identifying key verbs and nouns.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I Like School!".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Our Favorite School Activities" Circle Time Game (Interactive Group Game). The activity focuses on students listening to prompts and responding with simple words or gestures, encouraging sharing preferences in a low-pressure environment. It directly connects to the theme "I Like Math!" and the scenario "I Like School!".</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in a circle. The teacher places flashcards of various school activities ('math', 'games', 'art', 'music', 'playground') in the center.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher starts by saying, "I like math!" and points to the 'math' flashcard.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then asks a student, "Do you like math?" The student listens and responds with "Yes!" or "No!" and then points to a flashcard of an activity they like (e.g., points to 'games'). If the student is comfortable, they can say "I like games!" The teacher supports with modeling.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher guides the students to listen to each other's responses and preferences, encouraging simple interactions like nodding when someone likes the same activity.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">To make it a game, the teacher can say, "If you like games, stand up!" Students listen and respond with the correct action. This incorporates multi-step instructions and immediate physical responses based on listening comprehension.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher ensures that all target vocabulary for school activities is used and preferences are expressed.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher conducts a quick "Listen and Show" activity. The teacher places 3-4 flashcards of school activities on the board (e.g., 'math', 'music', 'playground', 'art'). The teacher then gives an instruction: "Point to the activity you like the most!", "Show me 'math'!", "Raise your hand if you like 'music'!", or "Do you like 'games' or 'art'?" (students point to their preference). Students respond individually with gestures, pointing, or simple 'Yes'/'No' or one-word answers. The teacher observes and takes notes on student comprehension.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension of instructions:</b> Student consistently responds with correct gestures/actions to oral commands (e.g., 'Point to math', 'Show me games').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Recognition of target vocabulary:</b> Student accurately identifies and points to 5+ target vocabulary words (e.g., 'math', 'games', 'art', 'music').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Response to Yes/No questions:</b> Student responds appropriately with 'Yes'/'No' or corresponding head gestures to preference questions (e.g., 'Do you like math?').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Engagement:</b> Student participates actively and maintains focus during listening activities.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> At the end of the lesson, the teacher asks students two simple questions:
            </p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you understand the games today?" (Students show thumbs up or down).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one school activity you like?" (Students point to a flashcard or say a simple word like 'math' or 'games').</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 4px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite game today?" (Students can point to a picture of the game or just say 'Bingo' or 'Simon Says').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you listen carefully to your friends?" (Students nod Yes or shake No).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening activities, especially "Bingo" and "Simon Says," worked very well for engaging Kinder students and ensuring active listening. The use of TPR and flashcards was crucial for comprehension. Some students needed more individual prompting for verbal responses, but all demonstrated comprehension through gestures and pointing. Next time, I will ensure even more explicit modeling of the /m/ sound in 'math' and the /g/ sound in 'games' during the initial stages. I also need to provide more opportunities for them to simply point to demonstrate understanding, rather than forcing verbal output too early. The pace was appropriate for the age group.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary related to school activities and basic phrases for expressing likes/dislikes through listening games. In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will engage with these same vocabulary words in a visual context by reading (or being read to and following along) a simple story about school activities to develop their early reading skills and connect written words to the sounds they've practiced.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
</table>